  "final_answer": "## Finding: a cost event was created 75 minutes after Pending Close\n\nThe supplied records identify manufacturing order **MO-25-09-1070**, work order **WO-2025-09-004**, for **Cooler Plate Pack** at Modine’s Racine operation.\n\n| Audit item | Recorded value |\n|---|---|\n| Source transaction | **TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070** |\n| Cost event | **CE-250904-0004** |\n| Event type | **INPUT — component issue** |\n| Amount | **USD $284,763.42** (28,476,342 cents) |\n| Event-created time | **2025-09-30T19:15:00** |\n| September Pending Close time | **2025-09-30T18:00:00** |\n| Time after Pending Close | **1 hour 15 minutes — 75 minutes** |\n| Recorded accounting status | **POSTED** |\n\nThe calculation is `19:15:00 − 18:00:00 = 4,500 seconds ÷ 60 = 75 minutes`. The timestamps are reproduced exactly as recorded; the source does not specify a timezone or UTC offset.\n\n### Policy conclusion\n\n**The recorded `POSTED` status is inconsistent with Oracle’s documented Pending Close treatment.** Oracle states that newly created transactions falling in or before a Pending Close period will not be staged in Cost Accounting or Receipt Accounting; they will be processed after the period is closed or reopened. Transactions created before the cutoff may continue processing. Revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments are exceptions, but this event is an **INPUT component issue**, not one of those exceptions.\n\n**Sources:** `work_orders`, `cost_periods`, and `work_order_cost_events` from the supplied Oracle SCM records; *Oracle Supply Chain Cost Management 26B*, “Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting,” printed page **225** (PDF page **233**).\n\nThe review was read-only; **no database records were changed**.",
